WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.120
A frozen UI. A sudden crash. If you build for iOS, your first instinct is to open Xcode.

2
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.080
But in a React Native app, standard native profiling tools often hit a brick wall.

3
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.840
React Native runs across two environments. The Hermes JavaScript thread and the iOS main thread,

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.280
separated by a rigid boundary, data must be serialized across.

5
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.040
Standard Swift tooling only understands one side. Xcode inspects the iOS thread while the

6
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.480
JavaScript side remains dark. When a crash originates inside the JavaScript runtime, those native tools

7
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.520
lose visibility. The diagnostic trace simply stops. Applying native debugging intuition

8
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.960
directly to React Native fails because the execution path vanishes the moment it crosses the bridge.

9
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.960
This leads to the specific frustration of clicking through an Xcode stack trace,

10
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.920
only to find empty, unreadable frames. This is a standard Xcode crash report,

11
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.600
prominently showing the underscore RCT fatal error. This is the opaque error handler that

12
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.240
surfaces when the Hermes engine aborts a process and triggers a native termination.

13
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.120
Xcode displays these unrecognizable HBC markers because it cannot parse the Hermes engines compiled

14
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.800
bytecode. Recovery visibility requires mapping existing native debugging intuition

15
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.040
to the specific architecture of the React Native 0.76 stack.

16
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.720
To do this, we need three specific instruments. The new React Native dev tools,

17
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.800
the flipper desktop app, and Xcode's time profiler. The following sections will demonstrate how to use

18
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:38.320
this integrated dual stack tool chain to recover the execution path. React Native 0.76 moves away

19
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.560
from the legacy flipper-based JavaScript debugger that earlier versions relied on.

20
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.240
This architecture diagram shows a first-party React Native dev tools built on the Chrome Dev

21
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:55.920
tools protocol. The Metro Bundler exposes port 8081, creating a direct CDP pipeline into the Hermes

22
00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.200
engine. This connection lets developers attach a diagnostic interface straight to the active

23
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.400
JavaScript runtime. When running development builds, the Metro Bundler automatically inlines

24
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.040
source maps directly into the active bundle. This allows you to set breakpoints in your

25
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.400
original type script files, rather than fighting to decipher minified output. Note that this modern

26
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:22.000
CDP workflow is distinct from the legacy Safari Web Inspector, which was restricted to the older

27
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.360
JavaScript core engine. By utilizing React Native dev tools, you restore stepping and expression

28
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.440
evaluation to the JS threat, rebuilding the first half of the execution stack. But React Native dev tools

29
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.960
cannot see everything. If a third-party native Swift SDK initiates a network request,

30
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:45.440
that traffic bypasses the central JS hub. These requests utilize native URL session via turbo modules,

31
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.760
because they occur outside of JavaScript, they never pass through the Hermes engine for dev tools

32
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:57.280
to inspect. To capture this native HTTP and HTTTS traffic, you must use Flipper's network plug-in as

33
00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.880
a dedicated interceptor. The second major blind spot lies in the calculation of visual component

34
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:09.520
frames. Look at these parallel wire frames. On one side is the JavaScript yoga-computed layout logic.

35
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.200
On the other are the final constraints rendered by the native iOS view hierarchy. When a layout breaks,

36
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:21.440
these two states often fail to align. Flipper's layout inspector serves as the react-specific version

37
00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.560
of Xcode's view hierarchy debugger, allowing you to compare the yoga calculations against the

38
00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.080
final native views. Flipper serves as a structural probe for inspecting network states and UI

39
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.840
constraints that fall into the gap between Hermes and UI kit. When an application stutters,

40
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:43.360
diagnosing dropped frames requires looking at the entire system at once. This is the Xcode

41
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.480
Instruments time-profiler. It is capable of overlaying CPU samples from both the native and

42
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:55.600
JavaScript environment simultaneously. Within the call-tree pane, you isolate two specific timelines,

43
00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:02.400
the main thread, where UI kit and fabric operate, and the JS thread, handling your react logic.

44
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:09.120
You can identify exactly which side is saturated. Heavy Swift or Objective-C frames stacking up here

45
00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:15.040
will block the main thread and drop frames. Conversely, heavy JavaScript work surfaces as dense

46
00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:22.640
blocks of JSContacts.EvaluateScript or Hermes runtime frames on the JS thread. Without post-processing,

47
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.000
the Hermes-Git compiled frames will appear in instruments as unreadable byte code markers.

48
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.920
If the performance data proves the bottleneck is purely on the JS thread,

49
00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.640
switch to the Hermes sampling profiler in React Native Dev Tools for readable JavaScript

50
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:42.080
function names. Performance tuning requires proving which thread is saturated before changing any

51
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:48.240
code. Transitioning to production, the JS stack often disappears entirely in crash reports.

52
00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:55.120
An uncaught JavaScript exception wraps into an at-rct fatal call, which then terminates the native iOS

53
00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:01.360
process. To catch this in development, you can attach LLDB to the running application. Setting

54
00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.680
a specific symbolic breakpoint for the at-rct fatal flag inside Xcode will catch the error handler

55
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:14.480
just before the process is destroyed. However, in release builds, this local LLDB tactic is unavailable,

56
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:19.440
because minification and ahead of time Hermes compilation fundamentally change the code structure.

57
00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:25.120
Since development breakpoints cannot be used in production, recovering the execution path requires

58
00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:30.640
rebuilding the stack externally. Production failures require a unified crash reporter integration,

59
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.680
such as Sentry or Firebase Crashlytics. This outlines the dual-symbolication requirement.

60
00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:41.600
First, the automated upload of Xcode's blue.dcm files. They resolve native frames,

61
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.480
turning cryptic hex addresses into readable native functions, leaving JavaScript garbled.

62
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:54.640
Second, Hermes source maps. The yellow.js bundle.map translates the byte code into readable type script.

63
00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:00.960
Maintaining this synchronized upload pipeline is necessary for matching raw native crash data

64
00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.720
with the JavaScript breadcrumbs that led to the failure. Effective react native debugging relies

65
00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.560
on understanding where the Hermes to iOS boundary lies. Solving these issues requires matching the

66
00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.800
diagnostic lens, dev tools, flipper, or instruments, to the specific side of the boundary where the logic

67
00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.800
failed.

